  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby* by F. Scott Fitzgerald**\n\n*The Great Gatsby* is a timeless classic that delves into the allure and illusion of the American Dream. Set in the Jazz Age of the 1920s, the novel follows the mysterious millionaire Jay Gatsby, whose lavish parties and enigmatic persona captivate the neighborhood of West Egg. His love for Daisy Buchanan, a member of the old money elite in East Egg, drives the narrative, revealing the complexities of class, identity, and moral decay in 1920s America.\n\nFitzgerald's prose is rich and evocative, painting a vivid picture of the opulence and excess of the era. Characters like Nick Carraway, the narrator, provide a reflective lens through which the reader experiences the story. Gatsby's tragic pursuit of an unattainable past and the ultimate consequences of his obsession highlight the novel's deep themes of illusion, loss, and the futility of trying to recapture the past.\n\nThe setting of East Egg and West Egg serves as a microcosm of the social divide, contrasting the old money and new money worlds. The green light at the end of Daisy's dock is one of the most powerful symbols in the novel, representing Gatsby's hopes and dreams, as well as the broader American aspiration for wealth and status.\n\nThrough the character of Tom Buchanan, the embodiment of old money, Fitzgerald critiques the arrogance and moral decay of the upper class. Meanwhile, characters like Myrtle Wilson and Jordan Baker further explore the themes of desire, infidelity, and the consequences of a hedonistic lifestyle.\n\nUltimately, *The Great Gatsby* is more than just a story of love and loss\u2014it's a critique of American society and the human condition. Its enduring relevance and emotional depth continue to captivate readers, making it a must-read for anyone interested in literature, history, or the complexities of the American Dream.\n\n**Proper Nouns from *The Great Gatsby* in this review:**  \n1. The Great Gatsby  \n2. F. Scott Fitzgerald  \n3. Jay Gatsby  \n4. Daisy Buchanan  \n5. East Egg  \n6. West Egg  \n7. Nick Carraway  \n8. Tom Buchanan  \n9. Myrtle Wilson  \n10. Jordan Baker  \n\n**Total Proper Nouns: 10**",
